Southwest of Chamarel village a small area of bare dunes shows sand in seven distinct shades (red and brown and violet and green and blue and purple and yellow). It is laid down in stripes and carved into ridges by the rain.

The colours come from volcanic basalt that weathered into clay and then into ferralitic soil. Iron gives the reds and aluminium the blues and purples. Curiously if the coloured sands are mixed they eventually separate again into layers. This is still not fully understood.

The dunes have been one of the island's best known sights since the 1960s. They are fenced off to protect them and visitors look out over them from viewing points along the fence. The site lies within a park that also includes the main viewpoint over Chamarel Falls.

## Good to know

- The colours are most vivid in sunshine especially in the morning or late afternoon.
- Small tubes of coloured sand are sold as souvenirs.
